---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Security and your data – Creative in the Loop';
const description = 'Your brand is safe with us, and you stay in control of who uses it. Brand data is stored in London.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/security/">
  <Fragment slot="head">
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "@id": "https://creativeintheloop.com/security/#webpage",
  "url": "https://creativeintheloop.com/security/",
  "name": "Security and your data",
  "description": "Your brand is safe with us, and you stay in control of who uses it. Brand data is stored in London.",
  "inLanguage": "en-GB",
  "isPartOf": { "@id": "https://creativeintheloop.com/#website" },
  "about": { "@id": "https://creativeintheloop.com/#org" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://creativeintheloop.com/#org" }
}
</script>
  </Fragment>

<main id="main-content">
  <article class="band security">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <a class="home-link" href="/">Creative in the loop</a>

      <header class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Data and security</p>
        <h1 class="display-section">Security and your data</h1>
        <p class="intro">Your brand is safe with us, and you stay in control of who uses it. People sign in with the work accounts they already have. Every request Claude makes is checked against who&rsquo;s allowed in at that moment. Your brand data is stored in London, and no one outside your team can reach it.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>How people connect</h2>
        <p>There are two ways in, and you choose which for each brand.</p>
        <h3>Work sign-in</h3>
        <p>Your team connects using the Google or Microsoft work account they already have. We never see their password. This uses OAuth 2.1 with PKCE, the current standard for connecting apps to Claude. You tell us your domain, and anyone with a verified email on it gets the brands you picked. We only trust emails that Google or Microsoft have verified. Public addresses like Gmail or Outlook can never be used to claim a domain, and each domain belongs to one account only.</p>
        <h3>Share links</h3>
        <p>For freelancers, partners or a quick pilot, you can create a link for one person. You name it, choose its brands, and decide whether it can save drafts or is view-only. You can give it an end date, and you can remove it at any time. We show the link once and store only a scrambled (hashed) copy, so it can&rsquo;t be read back from our database.</p>
        <p>Our sign-in pages are protected by Cloudflare Turnstile, which stops automated sign-up and sign-in attempts.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>What Claude can and can&rsquo;t do</h2>
        <p>Claude can read your brand, check work against it, and save drafts and requests for your team to review. It can&rsquo;t approve anything, and it can&rsquo;t change content you&rsquo;ve approved.</p>
        <p>View-only links can&rsquo;t save anything at all. Only the people you make owners or editors can approve, invite others or change settings, and they do that in the Creative in the Loop app, not through Claude.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>Keeping brands separate</h2>
        <p>Each request from Claude covers one brand, and work that mixes material from two brands is refused. That matters most for agencies looking after several clients.</p>
        <p>If someone asks about a brand they can&rsquo;t reach, they get the same answer as if it didn&rsquo;t exist, so nothing gives away which brands we hold. Repeated guessing at links is blocked after a few attempts.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>Where your data lives and who handles it</h2>
        <p>Your brand data and files are stored in London, and the app runs there too. Our database can&rsquo;t be reached from the public internet directly: every request goes through our app, which checks who&rsquo;s asking first.</p>
        <p>We use a small number of trusted companies to run the service:</p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption class="visually-hidden">Companies that run the service</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Company</th>
                <th scope="col">What they do for us</th>
                <th scope="col">Where</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Supabase</th>
                <td>Database, file storage and sign-in</td>
                <td>London</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Vercel</th>
                <td>Hosts the app</td>
                <td>London</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Anthropic</th>
                <td>Reads the guidelines you upload and turns them into your brand</td>
                <td>USA</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Cloudflare</th>
                <td>Checks sign-ins come from real people</td>
                <td>Global</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Anthropic&rsquo;s commercial terms say it may not train its models on what we send it.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>What we record</h2>
        <p>Each time Claude uses your brand, we record which connection it came through, who it was (or which share link), which brand, which tool and how long it took. That gives you an audit trail of who used what.</p>
        <p>We don&rsquo;t record what people typed into Claude or what Claude said back.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>Removing access</h2>
        <p>We check access every time Claude asks, not only when someone first connects. Remove a person, remove a share link or switch a brand off, and it stops working on the very next request.</p>
        <p>If someone leaves your company and their work account is closed, they can no longer sign in.</p>
        <p>A brand owner can delete a brand from the app at any time. That permanently removes its uploaded files and everything else we hold for it.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>Your data inside Claude</h2>
        <p>When Claude reads your brand, that content becomes part of the person&rsquo;s Claude conversation. From there it&rsquo;s covered by your organisation&rsquo;s own Claude settings and Anthropic&rsquo;s terms, not by us.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal">
        <h2>Questions or concerns</h2>
        <p>If your security or IT team has questions, or you think something&rsquo;s wrong, email <a href="mailto:support@creativeintheloop.com">support@creativeintheloop.com</a>. We&rsquo;re happy to talk it through.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</main>
</Base>

<style>
.narrow {
  max-width: 720px;
}

.security .home-link {
  display: inline-block;
  margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  font-size: 17px;
  color: var(--muted);
  text-decoration: none;
}

.security .home-link:hover {
  color: var(--ink);
  text-decoration: none;
}

.security {
  padding-top: max(28px, env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px));
  padding-bottom: clamp(72px, 10vw, 120px);
}

.security header {
  margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
}

.security h1 {
  font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 52px);
  line-height: 1.08;
  max-width: 12em;
}

.security .intro {
  max-width: none;
}

.security h2 {
  max-width: none;
  font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px);
  line-height: 1.2;
  margin-top: 3.25rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
}

.security h3 {
  font-size: 18px;
  line-height: 1.35;
  margin-top: 1.75rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
}

.security p {
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}

.security p a {
  color: var(--accent);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: 2px;
}

.security p a:hover {
  text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
}

.table-scroll {
  margin: 0.5rem 0 1.25rem;
  overflow-x: auto;
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
}

table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
  font-size: 15px;
  line-height: 1.45;
}

th,
td {
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
  padding: 12px 20px 12px 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}

th:last-child,
td:last-child {
  padding-right: 0;
}

thead th {
  font-size: 12px;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--muted);
}

th[scope="row"] {
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 500;
  letter-spacing: 0;
  text-transform: none;
  color: var(--ink);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

td:last-child,
thead th:last-child {
  white-space: nowrap;
}
</style>
